        "id": "REV-012",
        "severity": "high",
        "path": "PLAN.md — acceptance criterion 2 and “Дедупликация и порядок доставки”",
        "evidence": "Acceptance criterion 2 requires that an alert for an already-sent price/date/route combination is not repeated. The planned at-least-once workflow explicitly admits a duplicate when the process dies after Telegram accepts `sendMessage` but before `status='sent'` commits; restart sees `pending` and resends it. This is a direct unresolved contradiction, regardless of its stated rarity.",
        "fix": "Choose and state compatible semantics. Either weaken acceptance to permit at-least-once duplicates, or implement at-most-once suppression by durably marking before send and document the possible lost alert. Telegram cannot make the external send and SQLite status update exactly-once atomic."
      },
      {
        "id": "REV-013",
        "severity": "medium",
        "path": "PLAN.md — `check_route` decision order / pending-alert error path",
        "evidence": "The plan says `record_price` is always step 5 and that a `send_telegram_alert` exception escapes to the outer `main()` handler. Therefore, for an anomalous fetched entry whose Telegram request fails, execution exits before step 5 and the observation is not stored. This violates criterion 2’s “each observation is written to prices.db” exactly in an expected transient-failure case.",
        "fix": "Ensure `record_price` runs in a `finally`-style path after every successfully fetched entry, independently of alert-claim, send, or status-update failures. Keep alert delivery errors isolated from observation persistence."
      },
      {
        "id": "REV-014",
        "severity": "high",
        "path": "PLAN.md — `fetch_cheapest`, `send_telegram_alert`, and `main()`",
        "evidence": "The plan requires the process to keep polling after network/API failures but does not specify finite `requests` connect/read timeouts. Requests has no timeout by default. A stalled TCP connection or an endpoint that accepts a connection and never responds can block the sole sequential process indefinitely, preventing later routes and all future cycles without raising `RequestException`.",
        "fix": "Add configurable finite connect/read timeouts and pass them to every Travelpayouts and Telegram request. Log timeout failures through the existing per-route handling; test that timeout exceptions do not stop subsequent route processing."
      },
      {
        "id": "REV-015",
        "severity": "medium",
        "path": "PLAN.md — `build_purchase_link`",
        "evidence": "The canonical rule treats every value beginning with `http` as trusted and prefixes any other relative value with only `https://www.aviasales.com`. The plan’s cited API behavior describes a link code/path requiring a documented Aviasales search base; a raw code needs the search path as well, so the resulting URL can be non-working. Conversely, `http://attacker.example/...` passes the prefix check and is sent to the user as a purported ticket link if an upstream response is malformed or compromised.",
        "fix": "Parse URLs rather than use a string-prefix test. Allow only HTTPS absolute URLs on an explicit Aviasales allowlist; for relative API link codes, append to the documented Aviasales search base including its search path. Add tests for a raw code, a relative path, an allowed absolute URL, and a disallowed absolute URL."
